.TH ATLAS-FILE-XXD-STRINGS 1 "2026-11-23" "CyberSci Command Atlas" "Command Atlas"
.SH NAME
atlas\-file\-xxd\-strings \- The first three commands for any unknown file: what is it (\fBfile\fR), what are its bytes (\fBxxd\fR), what text is inside (\fBstrings\fR).
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
file   \-k  holiday.txt          # what do the first bytes say it is?
xxd    \-l 32  pic.png          # show the first 32 bytes in hex + ASCII
strings \-n 8 \-t x  hello       # printable runs of 8+ characters, with their hex offset
.fi
.SH WHEN TO USE
.IP \(bu 2
Triage every supplied file before you open or run it.
.IP \(bu 2
The extension and the contents disagree (a \fB.txt\fR that is really a PNG).
.IP \(bu 2
Find hidden or appended data, magic bytes, embedded paths and flags.
.SH FLAGS
.TP
\fBfile \-k\fR
Keep going: show all matches, not just the first.
.br
Use it when: Polyglots and odd formats.
.TP
\fBfile \-i\fR
MIME type output.
.br
Use it when: Scripts and quick filters.
.TP
\fBxxd \-l N / \-s OFFSET\fR
Read N bytes / start at an offset (negative = from the end).
.br
Use it when: Look at a header or a trailer.
.TP
\fBxxd \-c N / \-g N\fR
Bytes per line / group size.
.br
Use it when: Make the layout fit your screen.
.TP
\fBxxd \-p\fR
Plain hex without offsets.
.br
Use it when: Copy bytes into a script.
.TP
\fBxxd \-r \-p\fR
Reverse: hex text back into bytes.
.br
Use it when: Decode a hex string.
.TP
\fBstrings \-n N\fR
Minimum length of a string (default 4).
.br
Use it when: Cut noise.
.TP
\fBstrings \-a\fR
Scan the whole file (not only data sections).
.br
Use it when: Be sure you did not skip anything.
.TP
\fBstrings \-t x\fR
Print the offset of each string (hex).
.br
Use it when: Jump to the spot in \fBxxd\fR.
.TP
\fBstrings \-e l\fR
UTF\-16 little\-endian strings.
.br
Use it when: Windows text, memory images.
.SH RECIPES
.SS "1. What is this file really?"
\fBfile\fR reads the first bytes (magic numbers), not the name. \fBholiday.txt\fR is a copy of the PNG.
.PP
.nf
$ file hello pic.png holiday.txt notes.txt.gz access.log
.fi
.PP
.nf
hello:        ELF 64\-bit LSB pie executable, x86\-64, version 1 (SYSV), dynamically linked, interpreter /lib64/ld\-linux\-x86\-64.so.2, BuildID[sha1]=2f3fb8ed1f8be6
pic.png:      PNG image data, 1 x 1, 8\-bit/color RGB, non\-interlaced
holiday.txt:  PNG image data, 1 x 1, 8\-bit/color RGB, non\-interlaced
notes.txt.gz: gzip compressed data, was "notes.txt", last modified: Mon Nov 23 18:22:27 2026, from Unix, original size modulo 2^32 174
access.log:   ASCII text
.fi
.PP
The \fB.txt\fR file is identified as a PNG, the gzip as gzip, the binary as an ELF executable. Never trust an extension.
.SS "2. Look at a header"
\fB\-l 32\fR limits to 32 bytes. Left: offset. Middle: hex bytes. Right: printable ASCII.
.PP
.nf
$ xxd \-l 32 pic.png
.fi
.PP
.nf
00000000: 8950 4e47 0d0a 1a0a 0000 000d 4948 4452  .PNG........IHDR
00000010: 0000 0001 0000 0001 0802 0000 0090 7753  ..............wS
.fi
.PP
\fB89 50 4e 47\fR is the PNG signature (\fB.PNG\fR). Every format has one; learn the common ones (PK zip, \fB%PDF\fR, ELF \fB7f 45 4c 46\fR).
.SS "3. Look at the end of a file"
A negative \fB\-s\fR counts from the end. Data after the format's end marker is a classic hiding place.
.PP
.nf
$ xxd \-s \-40 pic.png
.fi
.PP
.nf
0000003f: 4e44 ae42 6082 6869 6464 656e 3a20 666c  ND.B`.hidden: fl
0000004f: 6167 7b64 656d 6f5f 6e6f 745f 615f 7265  ag{demo_not_a_re
0000005f: 616c 5f66 6c61 677d                      al_flag}
.fi
.PP
After the PNG end chunk (\fBIEND\fR) there is readable text that the picture viewer never shows.
.SS "4. Readable strings with offsets"
\fB\-t x\fR prints each string's offset in hex. The pipe keeps the interesting lines.
.PP
.nf
$ strings \-n 8 \-t x hello | grep \-E 'flag|sesame|denied'
.fi
.PP
.nf
   2004 flag{demo_not_a_real_flag}
   201f open\-sesame
   202b access denied
.fi
.PP
The secret text sits in the binary. Note that the passphrase \fBopen\-sesame\fR is visible as a string too: strings often reveals comparison values.
.SS "5. Convert hex text back to bytes"
\fB\-r \-p\fR reverses plain hex into bytes. The extra \fBecho\fR adds a newline.
.PP
.nf
$ echo '666c61677b6865787d' | xxd \-r \-p; echo
.fi
.PP
.nf
flag{hex}
.fi
.PP
Hex that looks random is often just ASCII. This one decodes to a flag\-shaped string.
.SS "6. UTF\-16 text hides from plain strings"
Windows stores text as 16\-bit characters, so there are NUL bytes between letters and plain \fBstrings\fR finds nothing.
.PP
.nf
$ printf 'p\e0a\e0s\e0s\e0w\e0o\e0r\e0d\e0' > wide.bin; strings wide.bin; echo '\-\-\- with \-e l:'; strings \-e l wide.bin
.fi
.PP
.nf
\-\-\- with \-e l:
password
.fi
.PP
The first command prints nothing; the second recovers \fBpassword\fR. Use \fB\-e l\fR on memory dumps and Windows artefacts.
.SH COMMON MISTAKES
.IP \(bu 2
\fBfile\fR can be wrong for polyglots (one file valid as two formats): use \fBfile \-k\fR and a format\-specific parser.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBstrings\fR shows only runs of printable characters; encoded or compressed data will not appear.
.IP \(bu 2
Hex dumps show bytes in file order; multi\-byte numbers in x86 files are little\-endian (\fBef be ad de\fR = \fB0xdeadbeef\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
Do not execute an unknown file to see what it does: analyse it first.
.SH PRACTICE
.IP 1. 3
Work out the real type of \fBholiday.txt\fR and extract the hidden text from its end.
.IP 2. 3
Find the byte offset of \fBflag{\fR in \fBhello\fR and view the bytes around it with \fBxxd \-s\fR.
.IP 3. 3
Decode \fBaGVsbG8gd29ybGQ=\fR using only commands from this page.
.SH SEE ALSO
binwalk, exiftool, hexdump \-C, od, readelf, objdump.
